\date{2025-11-03T00:05:19Z}
\title{Swarm typing judgments}
\tag{swarm}
\tag{note}

\import{swarm-macros}

\p{We present the typing rules in a bidirectional style.  There are two basic typing judgments.  The first is

##{\swinfer{\Gamma}{t}{\tau}{\Delta}}

where #{\Gamma} is a type context, #{\Delta} is a [capability context](000W), #{t} is a term, and #{\tau} is a [type](000Y).  This should intuitively be read as saying that #{t} can be inferred as having type #{\tau} in context #{\Gamma}, and can be evaluated given [capabilities](000X) #{\Delta}.  In this judgment #{\Gamma} and #{t} are considered inputs, and #{\tau} and #{\Delta} are considered outputs.
}

\p{Dually, ##{\swcheck{\Gamma}{t}{\tau}{\Delta}} means that #{t} can be \em{checked} to have type #{\tau} in context #{\Gamma}, and requires capabilities #{\Delta} to be evaluated.  Here, #{\Gamma}, #{t}, and #{\tau} are considered inputs, and #{\Delta} is still considered an output.}

\p{Note that the judgments only say something about the capabilities required to \em{evaluate} #{t}.  Further use of the resulting value may require additional capabilities: for example, evaluating the application of #{t} to an argument, if it is a function; or executing #{t}, if it is a command. As a rule, the capabilities needed for such uses beyond evaluation will be recorded in the type #{\tau}.}